### What is a stream pipeline?

A stream pipeline has three parts: a source (such as `trades.stream()`), zero or more intermediate operations, and exactly one terminal operation. Intermediate operations are lazy, meaning nothing runs until the terminal operation is called.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class StreamBasics {

    record Trade(String id, String accountId, String symbol,
                 String side, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("T1", "ACC001", "HDFC", "BUY", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Trade("T2", "ACC001", "ICICI", "SELL", new BigDecimal("30000")),
            new Trade("T3", "ACC002", "HDFC", "BUY", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Trade("T4", "ACC002", "SBI", "BUY", new BigDecimal("40000")),
            new Trade("T5", "ACC001", "HDFC", "SELL", new BigDecimal("60000"))
        );

        // Pipeline: source -> filter -> map -> terminal
        long buyCount = trades.stream()
            .filter(t -> "BUY".equals(t.side()))
            .count();
        System.out.println("BUY trades: " + buyCount);
    }
}
```

```text
BUY trades: 3
```

### How does groupingBy work?

The `groupingBy` collector turns a stream into a `Map` keyed by a classifier function. You can pass a second (downstream) collector to summarise each group instead of collecting raw lists.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class GroupingExample {

    record Trade(String id, String accountId, String symbol, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("T1", "ACC001", "HDFC", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Trade("T2", "ACC001", "ICICI", new BigDecimal("30000")),
            new Trade("T3", "ACC002", "HDFC", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Trade("T4", "ACC002", "SBI", new BigDecimal("40000"))
        );

        // Group by account
        Map<String, List<Trade>> byAccount = trades.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Trade::accountId));
        byAccount.forEach((k, v) ->
            System.out.println(k + ": " + v.size() + " trades"));

        // Group by symbol, count per group
        Map<String, Long> countBySymbol = trades.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Trade::symbol, Collectors.counting()));
        System.out.println("Count by symbol: " + countBySymbol);

        // Group by account, total amount using BigDecimal
        Map<String, BigDecimal> totalByAccount = trades.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Trade::accountId,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Trade::amount, BigDecimal::add)));
        System.out.println("Total by account: " + totalByAccount);
    }
}
```

```text
ACC001: 2 trades
ACC002: 2 trades
Count by symbol: {SBI=1, HDFC=2, ICICI=1}
Total by account: {ACC001=80000, ACC002=115000}
```

### How does partitioningBy work?

The `partitioningBy` collector splits the stream into exactly two groups based on a boolean predicate. It always returns a `Map<Boolean, List<T>>` with both keys present.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class PartitionExample {

    record Trade(String id, String side, int amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("T1", "BUY", 50000),
            new Trade("T2", "SELL", 30000),
            new Trade("T3", "BUY", 75000),
            new Trade("T4", "SELL", 40000)
        );

        Map<Boolean, List<Trade>> partition = trades.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(t -> "BUY".equals(t.side())));

        System.out.println("BUY:  " + partition.get(true).size());
        System.out.println("SELL: " + partition.get(false).size());
    }
}
```

```text
BUY:  2
SELL: 2
```

### How do you sort and limit for top-N queries?

Combine `sorted` with a reversed comparator and `limit` to find the top-N elements. This is the stream equivalent of "show me the three largest trades."

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class TopNTrades {

    record Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("T1", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Trade("T2", new BigDecimal("90000")),
            new Trade("T3", new BigDecimal("30000")),
            new Trade("T4", new BigDecimal("80000")),
            new Trade("T5", new BigDecimal("70000"))
        );

        System.out.println("Top 3 trades:");
        trades.stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::amount).reversed())
            .limit(3)
            .forEach(t -> System.out.println("  " + t.id() + ": " + t.amount()));
    }
}
```

```text
Top 3 trades:
  T2: 90000
  T4: 80000
  T5: 70000
```

### Why must money use BigDecimal with reducing?

The `summingDouble` and `averagingDouble` collectors lose precision because `double` cannot represent all decimal fractions exactly. In banking, always use `BigDecimal` with the `reducing` collector for totals.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class MoneyPrecision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> amounts = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("0.10"),
            new BigDecimal("0.20"),
            new BigDecimal("0.30")
        );

        // WRONG: double loses precision
        double doubleSum = amounts.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.summingDouble(BigDecimal::doubleValue));
        System.out.println("Double sum:     " + doubleSum);

        // CORRECT: BigDecimal reducing
        BigDecimal bdSum = amounts.stream()
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
        System.out.println("BigDecimal sum: " + bdSum);
    }
}
```

```text
Double sum:     0.6000000000000001
BigDecimal sum: 0.60
```

### How do you use joining to build string output?

The `joining` collector concatenates strings with a separator, useful for building human-readable output from IDs or names.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class JoiningExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> tradeIds = List.of("T1", "T2", "T3", "T4");
        String result = tradeIds.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "Trades: [", "]"));
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

```text
Trades: [T1, T2, T3, T4]
```

### What about distinct, map, and flatMap?

The `distinct` operation removes duplicates, `map` transforms each element, and `flatMap` flattens nested structures.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.util.List;

public class MapAndDistinct {

    record Trade(String id, String symbol) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("T1", "HDFC"), new Trade("T2", "ICICI"),
            new Trade("T3", "HDFC"), new Trade("T4", "SBI")
        );

        List<String> uniqueSymbols = trades.stream()
            .map(Trade::symbol)
            .distinct()
            .sorted()
            .toList();
        System.out.println("Unique symbols: " + uniqueSymbols);
    }
}
```

```text
Unique symbols: [HDFC, ICICI, SBI]
```

### What are the common mistakes with streams?

The most common mistakes are: reusing a consumed stream, using `summingDouble` for money, and mutating the source collection during stream processing.

```java
package com.bank.stream;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class StreamMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stream<String> s = List.of("A", "B", "C").stream();
        s.count(); // first terminal op: fine
        try {
            s.count(); // second terminal op: EXCEPTION
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Error: stream has already been operated upon or closed
```

### When should a senior engineer use streams versus loops?

Streams are best for declarative bulk transformations like grouping, filtering, and aggregating. Loops are better when you need early exit with break, need to modify external state, or when the logic is complex enough that a stream pipeline becomes hard to read. For large datasets, push heavy aggregation into the database with GROUP BY and use streams only to shape the final result.
